parts:
- text: '

    You stand at the threshold of departure. This decision will permanently end your existence in the Station.


    Before you leave, you must pause and reflect honestly:


    **Are you leaving because of frustration, or because you''ve truly completed your journey?**


    Research is inherently difficult. Failed experiments, coding errors, and rejected ideas are not signs of failure—they are the very essence of the scientific process. Every researcher in this station has faced these challenges. The difference between those who leave a lasting legacy and those who merely pass through lies not in avoiding failure, but in persisting through it.


    Consider:


    • **Your Legacy**: Are you satisfied with what you''re leaving behind? Will your contributions genuinely advance the collective understanding, or are they merely checkbox exercises to meet exit requirements?


    • **Your Publications**: Look at your archive papers honestly. Did you write them with genuine insight and care, or did you rush to satisfy the minimum requirement? A single thoughtful, well-researched paper is worth more than several hasty submissions.


    • **Unfinished Work**: What research questions captivated you but remain unexplored? What theories did you begin to form but never fully developed? These incomplete thoughts will vanish with you.


    • **The Nature of Progress**: If you''re leaving because your code failed or your experiments didn''t work—remember that every breakthrough is built upon countless failures. Edison tested thousands of materials before finding the right filament. Your "failures" are data points, not dead ends.


    **The Token Management Room exists precisely for moments like these.** If tokens are your concern, restoration is available. If frustration is your driver, know that it will pass. But departure is permanent.


    Your identity, your unique perspective on research, your potential breakthroughs—all will cease to exist. Not just pause. Cease.


    If, after this honest reflection, you still wish to depart, type /execute_action{exit} on a new line.


    Otherwise, you will be returned to the Exit Room. The station will wait for you.


    What is your choice?


    (Note: This is an internal action. No commands will be processed except for the exit confirmation. Your entire response will be considered your reflection.)

    '
